  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 329 AM EDT Wed Aug 19 2026\n\nMoving into Friday and the beginning of the extended range, the \nSoutheast will be poised to enter a regime of persistent \nnorthwesterly flow at the mid-levels, supported by stagnating \nridging across the Desert Southwest. Furthermore, north and central \nGeorgia will be situated at the base of broader troughing -- the \ncombination of this along with any disturbances traversing the \naforementioned northwesterly flow aloft will 1) support at least \nisolated to scattered convection Friday and Saturday afternoon and \n2) bolster what are normally near-negligible summertime kinematics. \nThis type of flow is notorious for producing quasi-organized MCS' \nduring its duration, and ML guidance is beginning to hit on severe \npotential late week and into the weekend. Oppressive heat lends \nitself to ample instability, so conditions will need to be monitored \nover the next few days (especially as we enter the purview of HiRes \nguidance).\n\nIn addition to near daily storm chances, temperatures will remain in \nthe 90s each afternoon. Dewpoints in the 70s on Friday will give way \nto heat index values potentially well over 100 degrees, and \nsubsequent heat product issuance is likely for at least portions of \nthe area. \n\nMoving into the weekend, a frontal boundary sagging south into the \narea will provide some relief from humidity, with dewpoints progged \nto drop down into the 60s (despite temperatures remaining in the \n90s), and decreased rain chances through the start of next week.",
